WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.466 --> 00:00:04.633
- And I was very, very, very
sure that what we were doing

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.718
was extremely important.

00:00:08.718 --> 00:00:10.989
We don't need a lot of grandstanders

00:00:10.989 --> 00:00:14.572
and people that want
the spotlight on them.

00:00:16.002 --> 00:00:19.294
I was willing to take
a chance because losing

00:00:19.294 --> 00:00:21.468
wasn't the worst thing in
the world that could happen.

00:00:21.468 --> 00:00:25.468
Nobody can do things by
themselves at any scale.

00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:31.436
For the first time in history
you were able to see inventory

00:00:31.436 --> 00:00:33.046
in motion or at rest.

00:00:33.046 --> 00:00:36.162
The important thing is to
think beyond your local economy

00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:39.206
to create higher margins
and increase growth

00:00:39.206 --> 00:00:40.164
for your business.

00:00:40.164 --> 00:00:42.064
All of a sudden you turn the kaleidoscope

00:00:42.064 --> 00:00:44.543
and there you see something
that nobody saw before.

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:47.507
Find yourself something you're good at

00:00:47.507 --> 00:00:49.007
and do it to perfection.

00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:50.242
What's the purple promise?

00:00:50.242 --> 00:00:52.916
And they'll say very
simply I think one thing.

00:00:52.916 --> 00:00:55.259
I will make every FedEx
experience outstanding.

00:00:55.259 --> 00:00:59.568
- He's the founder, chairman,
president, and CEO of FedEx.

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:02.204
In 2014, Fortune magazine ranked him

00:01:02.204 --> 00:01:05.930
number 26 on the world's
50 greatest leaders.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.641
He has an estimated net
worth of 3.6 billion dollars.

00:01:09.641 --> 00:01:11.665
He's Fred Smith, and here is my take

00:01:11.665 --> 00:01:14.127
on his top ten rules for success.

00:01:14.127 --> 00:01:16.859
(wind howling)
(basketball bouncing)

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:18.450
- The reason I never left confidence

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.023
is because I never believed
that the consequences of losing

00:01:23.023 --> 00:01:27.190
were as bad as some other
people might have thought.

00:01:28.441 --> 00:01:30.048
You know, oh my goodness,
I've lost my money

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:34.120
or what have you, I just wasn't
motivated along those lines

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.703
and I was very, very, very sure

00:01:38.276 --> 00:01:42.659
that what we were doing
was extremely important

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:45.606
and was destined to be successful.

00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:47.231
So that's the definition, I think,

00:01:47.231 --> 00:01:51.177
of an insane person or a zealot. (laughs)

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:55.030
And most entrepreneurs,
I think you would find,

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.052
have that sort of green wire laid in there

00:01:58.052 --> 00:02:02.135
just a little bit crosswise
and they begin to get

00:02:03.277 --> 00:02:07.795
focused on something and
they believe in the idea

00:02:07.795 --> 00:02:10.339
or themselves far beyond
what they probably should.

00:02:10.339 --> 00:02:12.899
We don't need a lot of grandstanders

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:16.482
and people that want
the spotlight on them.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.256
You know, all the way back to Sun Tzu,

00:02:21.256 --> 00:02:23.756
who made the famous quote that

00:02:25.413 --> 00:02:29.580
if you're a good leader,
the ultimate test of that

00:02:31.574 --> 00:02:34.755
is that the people will
accomplish great things

00:02:34.755 --> 00:02:37.161
and they'll think you had
nothing to do with it.

00:02:37.161 --> 00:02:39.386
So I think they have to be self-effacing,

00:02:39.386 --> 00:02:43.553
I think they have to have an
understanding of the mission.

00:02:46.389 --> 00:02:49.351
Whether it's football, or
in the military, or FedEx

00:02:49.351 --> 00:02:51.752
or what have you they
got to have that DNA.

00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:54.606
They've got to understand
those little tiny details

00:02:54.606 --> 00:02:57.515
and be able to synthesize them together,

00:02:57.515 --> 00:03:00.522
particularly at their organizations

00:03:00.522 --> 00:03:03.703
and that's a very important thing.

00:03:03.703 --> 00:03:06.285
Here's an inspiring story
from a recent edition

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:09.394
of the New York Times, for example.

00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:12.958
It was about a Michigan
company called Bandals

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:15.434
that introduced a new product.

00:03:15.434 --> 00:03:19.601
Women's sandals with
interchangeable decorative tops.

00:03:21.127 --> 00:03:22.765
And they introduced these products

00:03:22.765 --> 00:03:26.932
right after the economic
crisis hit in the fall of 2008.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.527
Pretty brave thing to start
a business in that time

00:03:32.527 --> 00:03:35.093
under any circumstances.

00:03:35.093 --> 00:03:37.885
When the five-employee company was pounded

00:03:37.885 --> 00:03:41.388
with manufacturing and
material cost increases

00:03:41.388 --> 00:03:45.044
of up to 30%, and
remember the price of fuel

00:03:45.044 --> 00:03:49.211
in the summer of 2008 went
to 147 dollars a barrel.

00:03:50.292 --> 00:03:53.432
The owner, Tom Sesti, decided that Bandals

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:56.642
needed to sell his way out of the problem

00:03:56.642 --> 00:03:58.965
and he made another
daring move in the midst

00:03:58.965 --> 00:04:02.248
of that economic crisis,
and as it turns out

00:04:02.248 --> 00:04:04.816
it was a very smart move, indeed.

00:04:04.816 --> 00:04:06.718
Now, in the Chinese language,

00:04:06.718 --> 00:04:10.486
the symbol for crisis and
opportunity are the same

00:04:10.486 --> 00:04:14.149
and this gave Mr. Sesti
the wake-up call he needed.

00:04:14.149 --> 00:04:18.316
He realized that because
Bandals sell best in warm months

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:22.010
expanding into overseas markets

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.121
with the year-round nice weather,

00:04:24.121 --> 00:04:26.936
or with seasons that ran
opposite to those here

00:04:26.936 --> 00:04:29.537
in the United States would be the way

00:04:29.537 --> 00:04:31.984
to dig his business out of a hole.

00:04:31.984 --> 00:04:33.924
After he did his homework and realized

00:04:33.924 --> 00:04:36.774
it would cost much less than he expected,

00:04:36.774 --> 00:04:40.586
he began selling Bandals in 15 countries.

00:04:40.586 --> 00:04:44.753
By 2011 the company's revenue
had increased a whopping 250%

00:04:47.541 --> 00:04:49.874
and his profits had tripled.

00:04:50.726 --> 00:04:54.547
I think that's a great story
about a small business person

00:04:54.547 --> 00:04:56.896
having the vision to see how his product

00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:00.469
can appeal to global markets,
and how the technology

00:05:00.469 --> 00:05:04.636
and supply chain advancements
we have today make it easier

00:05:05.564 --> 00:05:08.952
and more cost-effective to
branch into those markets.

00:05:08.952 --> 00:05:11.863
In retrospect, it was ridiculous to try

00:05:11.863 --> 00:05:14.196
to put this system together,

00:05:17.643 --> 00:05:19.844
which required so much up front money

00:05:19.844 --> 00:05:22.745
and required changing a lot
of government regulations,

00:05:22.745 --> 00:05:25.012
but I didn't know that at the time.

00:05:25.012 --> 00:05:29.422
And I think probably my
experience in the service

00:05:29.422 --> 00:05:33.589
where the currency of the
exchange in FedEx was just money.

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:39.261
You know, it wasn't people's
arms and legs or lives,

00:05:39.261 --> 00:05:43.428
and so my perspective on
it was perhaps a bit more,

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:48.562
I don't know how you would say it.

00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:51.962
I was willing to take a chance

00:05:51.962 --> 00:05:54.850
because losing wasn't
the worst thing the world

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:57.481
that could happen to you, I
had seen that very clearly.

00:05:57.481 --> 00:06:01.398
Nobody can do things by
themselves at any scale

00:06:04.366 --> 00:06:08.533
and if you read about Marshall,
Marshall was extraordinarily

00:06:10.854 --> 00:06:14.416
able to deal with difficult personalities

00:06:14.416 --> 00:06:17.538
and to synthesize them into a team.

00:06:17.538 --> 00:06:20.065
Admiral King, who was the
Chief of Naval Operations

00:06:20.065 --> 00:06:22.213
at the time was a very prickly fellow.

00:06:22.213 --> 00:06:24.796
He had a very tough personality

00:06:26.104 --> 00:06:30.271
and he was found to be
intractable on many, many things,

00:06:33.384 --> 00:06:34.912
but Marshall could deal with him

00:06:34.912 --> 00:06:37.821
and he did it in many different ways,

00:06:37.821 --> 00:06:39.855
many times being self-effacing

00:06:39.855 --> 00:06:43.151
by walking over to King's
office and so forth.

00:06:43.151 --> 00:06:47.069
Now, he ended up being in charge
of the whole shooting match

00:06:47.069 --> 00:06:49.636
and Roosevelt's architect of the war,

00:06:49.636 --> 00:06:53.649
but in the early stages, he had
to build that team synthesis

00:06:53.649 --> 00:06:56.161
to get things done, and
he was a master at it.

00:06:56.161 --> 00:07:00.853
We handle millions and
millions of pieces every day

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:03.243
and one of the things we did years ago,

00:07:03.243 --> 00:07:06.196
which was very important,
is we figured out

00:07:06.196 --> 00:07:08.158
our customers wanted the information

00:07:08.158 --> 00:07:10.500
about where every one
of 'em was every day,

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.098
which was impossible at the time,

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:13.990
but we had an internal entrepreneur

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.524
that figured out how to do it.

00:07:15.524 --> 00:07:17.345
He revolutionized all kinds of things.

00:07:17.345 --> 00:07:18.855
It's a great story, I think it's in

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:21.788
the National Academy of Sciences

00:07:21.788 --> 00:07:23.518
about how the FedEx people did it.

00:07:23.518 --> 00:07:26.030
And the reason it was so important,

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.469
for the first time in history

00:07:27.469 --> 00:07:32.137
you were able to see inventory
in motion or at rest.

00:07:32.137 --> 00:07:34.727
Now, a warehouse doesn't add any value.

00:07:34.727 --> 00:07:37.090
It's just a place to put
something so you know you got it.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.360
Well, once we invented the
tracking of things in motion

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.216
you could keep track of inventory

00:07:42.216 --> 00:07:44.447
whether it was moving
in a 500 mile an hour

00:07:44.447 --> 00:07:47.133
or 550 triple seven,
or in a 50 mile an hour

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:50.557
FedEx over the road truck,
and it allowed people

00:07:50.557 --> 00:07:53.140
to have fantastic productivity.

00:07:54.292 --> 00:07:58.057
It's what allowed, along
with government deregulation

00:07:58.057 --> 00:08:01.390
of transportation, things like Wal-Mart,

00:08:02.436 --> 00:08:06.603
which is really a logistics
system, not just a retailer.

00:08:08.236 --> 00:08:10.134
With the help of the internet,

00:08:10.134 --> 00:08:13.286
and global logistics like we provide,

00:08:13.286 --> 00:08:15.556
more small business have become part of

00:08:15.556 --> 00:08:19.723
the global supply chain than
at any other time in history.

00:08:21.241 --> 00:08:25.158
52% of all small businesses are home-based

00:08:26.495 --> 00:08:28.456
and I think it's fair to say that people

00:08:28.456 --> 00:08:31.668
are selling goods to the national
and international markets

00:08:31.668 --> 00:08:35.013
that they could have only
sold locally 20 years ago

00:08:35.013 --> 00:08:36.680
in many, many cases.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.110
Now, whether you're expanding
nationally or internationally

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.303
the important thing is to
thing beyond your local economy

00:08:44.303 --> 00:08:46.407
to create higher margins

00:08:46.407 --> 00:08:48.732
and increase growth for your business.

00:08:48.732 --> 00:08:52.899
I think some people just see
things that others don't.

00:08:57.129 --> 00:09:00.269
There was a great professor
at your alma mater,

00:09:00.269 --> 00:09:03.436
I hate to say it, Rosabeth Moss Kanter

00:09:05.141 --> 00:09:08.074
and Professor Kanter used to describe it

00:09:08.074 --> 00:09:12.070
as kaleidoscope thinking,
that you look at something

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.600
and it's a pattern and then
you turn the kaleidoscope

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:18.259
and it's another pattern
and it's meaningless

00:09:18.259 --> 00:09:20.224
and all of a sudden you
turn the kaleidoscope

00:09:20.224 --> 00:09:23.597
and there you see something
that nobody saw before.

00:09:23.597 --> 00:09:27.764
I mean, clearly lots of
people fit into that category.

00:09:29.162 --> 00:09:31.939
Steve Jobs, you see it with the products

00:09:31.939 --> 00:09:34.749
that he's come out with and
Bill Gates and so forth.

00:09:34.749 --> 00:09:38.492
I just think that General
Marshall in his field

00:09:38.492 --> 00:09:41.992
was able to synthesize all of those things

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:46.494
that he learned in France
and the experiences

00:09:46.494 --> 00:09:50.227
that he had in the army to
understand the great crisis

00:09:50.227 --> 00:09:53.621
that was ahead and to
prepare for it uniquely.

00:09:53.621 --> 00:09:55.269
And for those of you who don't know it,

00:09:55.269 --> 00:09:58.860
he ran the army school at Fort Benning

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.021
and I think at Leavenworth, too

00:10:01.021 --> 00:10:03.918
and he compiled a list of officers

00:10:03.918 --> 00:10:06.668
that he felt in time of emergency

00:10:07.579 --> 00:10:09.774
would be the ones that he should promote

00:10:09.774 --> 00:10:11.747
if he were put in that position.

00:10:11.747 --> 00:10:14.728
And he was put in that position
and he did promote them

00:10:14.728 --> 00:10:18.120
with fantastic success over the heads

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.906
of many, many other
officers who were seniors

00:10:20.906 --> 00:10:23.317
to the ones that had impressed him.

00:10:23.317 --> 00:10:27.204
So he was able to see that
however it came about.

00:10:27.204 --> 00:10:31.195
I thought many times about
the advice I might give

00:10:31.195 --> 00:10:35.575
a graduating class, and I
can find no better quote

00:10:35.575 --> 00:10:39.850
than one that was given some 200 years ago

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.017
by a Polish general Tadeusz Kosciuszko

00:10:45.397 --> 00:10:46.689
who had come to the United States

00:10:46.689 --> 00:10:48.764
to help Washington along with Lafayette

00:10:48.764 --> 00:10:50.641
win our independence.

00:10:50.641 --> 00:10:53.098
He was an engineer, fortified West Point

00:10:53.098 --> 00:10:57.265
and was a major contributor to
the success of American arms.

00:10:58.496 --> 00:11:02.704
He said shortly after the
war to a group of students

00:11:02.704 --> 00:11:06.871
such as yourself, "Set yourself
to work with reflection

00:11:07.948 --> 00:11:12.124
"and intelligence either
at your prescribed duty,

00:11:12.124 --> 00:11:15.893
"carry it out in the
most scrupulous manner,

00:11:15.893 --> 00:11:18.698
"or perfect yourself in some science

00:11:18.698 --> 00:11:22.031
"in which you should have true mastery."

00:11:22.929 --> 00:11:24.433
Perhaps in a shortened version,

00:11:24.433 --> 00:11:27.774
General Colin Powell summed it up,

00:11:27.774 --> 00:11:30.004
"Find yourself something you're good at

00:11:30.004 --> 00:11:32.171
"and do it to perfection."

00:11:33.183 --> 00:11:36.003
Something you enjoy, and if you do that,

00:11:36.003 --> 00:11:40.715
I can promise you, as I have
over the past 30-some odd years

00:11:40.715 --> 00:11:43.748
you won't feel that you've gone
to work a day in your life.

00:11:43.748 --> 00:11:46.211
FedEx over its entire existence, David,

00:11:46.211 --> 00:11:48.734
has gotten better and better
and better at what we do

00:11:48.734 --> 00:11:51.704
and we've been able to do
that for three reasons.

00:11:51.704 --> 00:11:54.110
One, the use of technology,
'cause just imagine

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:58.347
if we were handling all
those people of things a day,

00:11:58.347 --> 00:11:59.804
and we couldn't keep track of them.

00:11:59.804 --> 00:12:03.517
I mean, things would be going
to Mars, for goodness sake.

00:12:03.517 --> 00:12:06.434
Secondly, we have availed ourselves

00:12:07.498 --> 00:12:11.665
of the most advanced scientific
quality management systems

00:12:12.607 --> 00:12:16.774
pioneered by Japanese industry
and led by Professor Deming

00:12:18.783 --> 00:12:21.881
and Professor Juran 25, 30 years ago.

00:12:21.881 --> 00:12:24.928
And third, we have a
culture which is represented

00:12:24.928 --> 00:12:27.625
by very a very simple phrase.

00:12:27.625 --> 00:12:30.298
We call it our purple promise.

00:12:30.298 --> 00:12:34.019
Purple is the common color
for all of the FedEx branding.

00:12:34.019 --> 00:12:36.468
We're a little like Coke,
you know, red's Coke Classic,

00:12:36.468 --> 00:12:39.687
and silver is Diet Coke and Gold is,

00:12:39.687 --> 00:12:41.890
I don't know what it is, but
it doesn't have much in it.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:45.324
(audience laughing)
I drink it, but.

00:12:45.324 --> 00:12:48.414
So, purple and orange is our
original express company,

00:12:48.414 --> 00:12:49.870
purple and platinum is corporate,

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.943
purple and green is our
ground parcel company,

00:12:51.943 --> 00:12:54.085
purple and blue, FedEx Office,

00:12:54.085 --> 00:12:57.169
and purple and crimson is FedEx Freight.

00:12:57.169 --> 00:12:59.391
So we have a very simple
phrase that every member

00:12:59.391 --> 00:13:01.528
of the FedEx team knows
and I hope if you walk up

00:13:01.528 --> 00:13:04.151
to somebody you ask them,
what's the purple promise?

00:13:04.151 --> 00:13:06.125
And they'll say very
simply, I think one thing,

00:13:06.125 --> 00:13:09.489
I will make every FedEx
experience outstanding.

00:13:09.489 --> 00:13:13.142
So you don't need 18 manuals
and one thing or another.

00:13:13.142 --> 00:13:14.649
You give them the best technology

00:13:14.649 --> 00:13:18.699
and try to support 'em, and
the best management tools

00:13:18.699 --> 00:13:21.116
and our team will deliver it.

00:13:22.077 --> 00:13:23.376
So we've gotten better
and better every year

00:13:23.376 --> 00:13:26.141
so we don't lose many of anyone's packages

00:13:26.141 --> 00:13:28.183
most, including mine.

00:13:28.183 --> 00:13:29.417
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:29.417 --> 00:13:32.557
I made this video because
girohead asked me to.

00:13:32.557 --> 00:13:33.887
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:33.887 --> 00:13:35.400
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.898
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:37.898 --> 00:13:39.529
I'd also love to know which of Fred Smith

00:13:39.529 --> 00:13:42.183
top ten rules had the
deepest impact on you.

00:13:42.183 --> 00:13:44.815
Leave it in the comments and
I will join in the discussion.

00:13:44.815 --> 00:13:47.557
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:13:47.557 --> 00:13:49.053
and I'll see you soon.

00:13:49.053 --> 00:13:51.526
(wind blowing)
(basketball bouncing)

